<DocsLayout title="Architecture">
  <h1>Architecture</h1>
  <p>
    Identity is a stateless Connect-RPC server. All durable state lives in the
    datastore (Postgres or SQLite). The container does five things on startup,
    then serves requests.
  </p>

  <h2>Process layout</h2>
  <Code code={`Browser / SDK
     |
     |  Connect-RPC over HTTP/JSON  (port 80)
     v
+-------------------------------+        +---------------------+
| Identity container            | -----> |                     |
|                               |        |     Datastore       |
|  Logging -> Recover -> Health |        | (Postgres / SQLite) |
|   -> Project -> CORS          |        |                     |
|   -> Client-IP -> Rate-limit  |        |                     |
|   -> JWKS -> Auth             |        |                     |
|   -> Project-guard -> Metrics |        |                     |
|   -> service.IdentityHandler  |        |                     |
+-------------------------------+        +---------------------+
     |
     |  Prometheus  (port 9090)
     v
  /metrics`} lang="plaintext" />

  <h2>Layers</h2>
  <p>
    Concrete files live under <code>internal/</code> and <code>pkg/</code>.
    Each layer has a single responsibility:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>cmd/identity/main.go</code> — process bootstrap, config and key-ring loading, listener startup, graceful shutdown.</li>
    <li><code>internal/app/app.go</code> — the <code>Build</code> function assembles the middleware chain (project resolver, project-scope guard, auth, etc.) and the Connect handler.</li>
    <li><code>internal/middleware/</code> — logging, CORS, health, JWKS endpoint, bearer-token auth.</li>
    <li><code>internal/connect/</code> — Connect-RPC adapter that translates between proto types and the service-layer Go types.</li>
    <li><code>internal/service/</code> — business logic. <code>AuthService</code>, <code>AdminService</code>, <code>GroupService</code>, <code>HelpService</code>, <code>ProfileService</code>. None of these import Connect or proto types.</li>
    <li><code>internal/repo</code> — backends behind the <code>service.Repository</code> / <code>service.DB</code> interfaces, picked by <code>GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER</code>: <code>postgres</code> (production, multi-node), <code>sqlite</code> (embedded / single-node / dev, pure-Go <code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>), and <code>memory</code> (tests / smoke). The <code>conformance</code> sub-package runs the same suite of behavioural tests against every backend so semantics (uniqueness, ordering, error translation) don't drift.</li>
    <li><code>pkg/jwt</code> — <code>KeyRing</code>, <code>SigningKey</code>, <code>CreateAccessToken</code>, <code>VerifyAccessToken</code>, JWKS marshalling.</li>
    <li><code>pkg/passwords</code> — bcrypt hashing (cost 12) + a strength validator.</li>
    <li><code>pkg/passkeys</code> — WebAuthn (<code>go-webauthn/webauthn</code>) wrapper.</li>
    <li><code>pkg/totp</code> — RFC 6238 TOTP + AES-GCM encryption for secrets at rest.</li>
    <li><code>pkg/audit</code> — append-only logger that records <code>AuditEvent</code> rows through the repository.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Middleware chain</h2>
  <p>
    The chain is built in <code>internal/app/app.go</code> (the
    <code>Build</code> function). Outermost runs first on the request path:
  </p>
  <Code code={`logging -> recover -> health -> project -> CORS -> client-IP -> rate-limit -> JWKS -> auth -> project-guard -> metrics -> Connect handler`} lang="plaintext" />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>logging</strong> — structured zap logs with request ID, latency, status.</li>
    <li><strong>recover</strong> — turns a panicking handler into a 500 instead of crashing the process.</li>
    <li><strong>health</strong> — answers <code>/health</code> and <code>/healthz</code> as the outermost functional hop, so liveness/readiness probes short-circuit before any per-request work (including the project resolver's Host lookup).</li>
    <li><strong>project</strong> — the project resolver maps the request (by credential or Host) to a project and pins it into context. This is the entry point of the Project/Tenant isolation model; it pins the default project when no control-plane resolver is wired.</li>
    <li><strong>CORS</strong> — layers the resolved project's per-project allow-list on top of the global floor from <code>GATEWAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS</code>, and short-circuits the OPTIONS preflight before rate-limit.</li>
    <li><strong>client-IP</strong> — resolves the caller IP from trusted proxies; must precede rate-limit, which keys on the resolved IP.</li>
    <li><strong>rate-limit</strong> — per-IP request throttling.</li>
    <li><strong>JWKS</strong> — serves <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code> straight from the in-memory <code>KeyRing</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>auth</strong> — verifies bearer tokens and writes the user ID to <code>X-Authenticated-User-Id</code>. Skipped for the exempt-path list (signup, login, JWKS, health, etc.).</li>
    <li><strong>project-guard</strong> — runs just after auth (which surfaces the verified project) and rejects an access token replayed across projects, enforcing project-scope isolation.</li>
    <li><strong>metrics</strong> — sits just outside the Connect mux so it observes every RPC's final status, including failures synthesized by the otelconnect interceptor.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Graceful shutdown</h2>
  <p>
    On <code>SIGTERM</code> or <code>SIGINT</code>, the server stops accepting
    new connections, waits up to 10 seconds for in-flight RPCs, then closes
    the datastore connection. The metrics server runs in its own goroutine and is
    cleaned up by process exit.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
